Title: tests that drive processes and sockets fail intermittently (two instances)
Status: open
Found: 2026-09-06, in passing, running the suite for an unrelated change

SYMPTOM

    ALDB-LISTENER-DRIVES-A-LIVE-SOCKET-SESSION in CLAUTOLISP-TOOL-SUITE:
      Unexpected Error: #<SB-INT:BROKEN-PIPE ...>
      Couldn't write to #<SB-SYS:FD-STREAM for
        "socket 127.0.0.1:44119, peer: 127.0.0.1:46746">:
        Broken pipe.

One failure out of 33 suites. The IMMEDIATELY FOLLOWING run of the same
suite, same image, same tree, was green: 33 suites, 0 failures. Nothing
between the two runs changed.

WHY IT IS WORTH A TICKET RATHER THAN A SHRUG

A test that fails once in a while is worse than one that fails always.
It teaches people to re-run rather than to look, and the day it fails
for a REAL reason it will be re-run too. This project has already spent
days on the opposite failure -- a silence read as evidence -- and a
flake is the same coin: an outcome nobody trusts enough to act on.

It also lands in CI, where a red pipeline on a merge request costs a
maintainer's attention before anyone knows it was noise.

WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE

The test drives a live aldb listener over a loopback socket. A broken
pipe on write means the peer closed first -- so the likely shape is a
race between the client finishing (or erroring) and the server writing
its next line, rather than anything about the protocol's content. Under
load, which is when a full suite run is at its slowest, the window
widens.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: run that test alone in a loop, a few hundred
times, and see whether the failure rate matches the load hypothesis.
Cheap, since it needs no CAD and no runner.

WHAT NOT TO DO: mark it flaky and retry it. That converts a race into a
slower race and keeps the untrustworthy outcome. Either the close is
ordered and the test should wait for it, or the writer should tolerate
a closed peer as a normal end -- and which of those is right is a
statement about the listener, not about the test.

NOT INTRODUCED BY THE COMPILER BRANCH: it appeared while running the
suite for a documentation change, on a tree whose only code delta was
one new compiler test, and the listener is master's work. Recorded here
rather than in that branch's issues because it belongs to the debugger
UI, not the compiler.


SECOND INSTANCE -- 2026-09-09, and it held up a release step
-------------------------------------------------------------

test:alfe:sbcl went red on the POST-RELEASE BUMP's pipeline, six
failures in AUTOLISP-FRONT-END-SUITE:

  KILL-ENGINE-PROCESS-IS-BOUNDED-NOT-BLOCKING
      "a TERM-ignoring process survived; :urgent did not fire"
  PROTOCOL-MOCK-CAD-FULL-LIFECYCLE

THE COMMIT UNDER TEST CHANGED TWO VERSION STRINGS. Nothing else. And
the master pipeline, on effectively the same tree, was clean: 15
success, 0 failed. Retried once: success. So it is neither
pre-existing nor caused by the change.

WHAT IT COST, which is why this is not merely noise: merge-when-
pipeline-succeeds does not fire on a red pipeline, so the post-release
bump did not land, and master went on claiming 2.2.1 -- a version that
was by then FROZEN AND PUBLISHED. Step 5 of the release procedure
exists precisely to prevent that, and a flake defeated it. It took a
human-noticed retry to finish a release.

THE PATTERN, which is worth more than either instance:

  ALDB-LISTENER-DRIVES-A-LIVE-SOCKET-SESSION   loopback socket, broken pipe
  KILL-ENGINE-PROCESS-IS-BOUNDED-NOT-BLOCKING  child process, signal escalation
  PROTOCOL-MOCK-CAD-FULL-LIFECYCLE             background thread + protocol

All three drive something that lives outside the Lisp image -- a
socket, a child process, a thread -- and all three assert about WHEN it
responds. That is the shape to look at, rather than three separate
mysteries. Under a full suite run, which is when the machine is
slowest, every such window is at its narrowest.

WHAT NOT TO DO, restated because a release is now involved and the
temptation is stronger: do not mark them flaky and retry automatically.
That converts a race into a slower race and keeps an outcome nobody can
act on -- and here the outcome gates a step that must not be skipped.
Either the wait is ordered and the test should wait for it, or the
timeout is too tight for a loaded machine and should say so in its
failure message.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: run the three in a loop, a few hundred times
each, on a loaded box. Cheap -- no CAD, no runner -- and it turns
"sometimes" into a rate that can be compared against a hypothesis.
